WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.440
A playbook usually begins with a signal. The signal might be an automated alert,

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:11.460
a user report, a suspicious observation by IT, a vendor notification, a customer question,

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:13.280
or a message from a partner.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:19.260
The first job is not to overreact or underreact. The first job is triage:

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:23.640
establish what is known, what is suspected, and what is still unknown.

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:27.780
Good triage questions are simple but powerful. What happened?

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.520
Which users, systems, accounts, data,

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:36.220
and business services may be involved? Is the activity confirmed, suspected,

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:37.880
or unverified?

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.300
Is the incident active right now? Is containment urgent?

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:45.520
What evidence supports the current assessment? What evidence is missing?

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:48.860
Who owns the affected assets? Has this happened before?

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:53.480
Does the signal match a known detection rule, a known false positive pattern,

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.140
or a new condition?

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:59.680
Consider a phishing report. The first signal may be one user's forwarded email.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:03.740
Triage asks whether other users received it, whether anyone clicked,

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:07.640
whether credentials were submitted, whether suspicious logins followed,

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:10.900
and whether the email contains a malicious attachment or link.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:16.160
For malware, triage asks which endpoint alerted, what process or file was involved,

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:20.940
whether the host is still connected, and whether other systems show related telemetry.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:26.120
For credential misuse, triage looks at authentication logs, location changes,

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:31.620
impossible travel patterns, new devices, failed login bursts, role changes,

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:33.920
or unusual access to data.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:39.660
For cloud account abuse, triage may focus on audit logs, new keys, policy changes,

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:42.920
resource creation, or public exposure changes.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:45.140
These examples stay defensive:

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:49.520
the purpose is to understand impact and protect the organization.

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:52.060
Severity assignment is a decision aid,

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:56.440
not a substitute for analysis. A severity label helps route the incident,

00:01:56.660 --> 00:02:00.100
determine response time, and involve the right stakeholders.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:05.600
It should consider scope, business impact, data sensitivity, active threat behavior,

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:10.700
containment urgency, legal or privacy implications, and confidence in the facts.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:15.720
A single infected test laptop may not carry the same severity as confirmed credential

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:17.940
misuse against a privileged account.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.120
A public data exposure involving sensitive records may require faster escalation than a

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.980
low-confidence alert on a nonproduction system.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:30.840
The playbook should define how severity can change. Early severity may be provisional

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:33.060
because the team has incomplete facts.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:38.480
As evidence improves, severity can go up or down. That change should be documented.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:44.700
Responders should use careful language: observed, suspected, confirmed, contained,

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:45.960
recovered,

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:50.740
unresolved. These words reduce confusion and help stakeholders understand the difference

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:53.040
between evidence and assumption.

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:59.340
Good triage ends with an actionable next step. Continue analysis, escalate, contain,

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:04.120
collect evidence, notify a stakeholder, or close as benign with documentation.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:08.340
The playbook should make that transition clear so the team does not stay stuck in endless

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:12.800
investigation or jump to containment without understanding what is at risk.
